# ComposeIO Node-RED Integration [SPEC:CIO-NODERED]

> Node-RED is a flow-based programming tool with excellent MQTT support and a
> visual wiring model that maps naturally to ComposeIO's namespace-to-namespace
> binding pattern. This spec defines how Node-RED participates on the ComposeIO
> bus as a first-class citizen: subscribing to namespace state changes, publishing
> state and events, validating against state engines, and bridging ComposeIO
> namespaces to the broader Node-RED ecosystem (HTTP endpoints, dashboards,
> home automation, databases, notification services).

---

## 1. Architecture Decision: Hybrid Integration [NR-ARCH]

### 1.1 The Three Options Evaluated [NR-ARCH-OPTIONS]

| Approach | Pros | Cons | Verdict |
|----------|------|------|---------|
| **Raw MQTT** | Zero custom code. Node-RED's mqtt-in/mqtt-out nodes work today with `composeio/{scope}/{namespace}` topics. | No state engine validation. No message format enforcement. No namespace discovery. No log mode awareness. Developer must manually construct JSON payloads. | Insufficient |
| **Direct bus library** | Full protocol awareness. Embeds the ComposeIO Python bus library (or a JS port) into Node-RED. | Massive implementation effort. Python library cannot run in Node-RED's JS runtime. A JS port duplicates 3,000+ lines of protocol logic. Maintenance burden doubles. | Overkill |
| **Hybrid: MQTT transport + semantic overlay nodes** | MQTT handles transport (zero custom transport code). Custom nodes add ComposeIO message formatting, state engine validation, namespace discovery, and log mode awareness on top. | Requires custom node development (moderate effort). State engine definitions must be loaded from config. | **Selected** |

### 1.2 Design Rationale [NR-ARCH-RATIONALE]

The hybrid approach respects three ComposeIO principles:

- **[DP-CIO-6]** (transport is an implementation detail): MQTT is already the primary networked transport. Node-RED nodes use the same `composeio/{scope}/{namespace}` topic structure as any other MQTT participant. No protocol changes needed.
- **[DP-CIO-25]** (proposed in [AMEND:BP-EXT]: services are bus participants, not bus extensions): Node-RED joins the bus as a participant. It publishes to namespaces, subscribes to namespaces, and follows the bus protocol. Node-RED-specific concerns (flow wiring, dashboard rendering, HTTP integration) stay inside Node-RED.
- **[DP-CIO-7]** (configuration is the product): The custom nodes load namespace definitions and state engines from `.compose.yaml` files or from bus discovery messages. The flow developer configures which namespaces to watch — the node handles the wire format.

### 1.3 Integration Topology [NR-ARCH-TOPOLOGY]

```
mqtt-broker.local
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                         |
|  MQTT Broker (:1883)                                    |
|    |                                                    |
|    +-- composeio/global/ftl_arm                         |
|    +-- composeio/global/engine_rpm                      |
|    +-- composeio/global/_bus/discovery                  |
|    +-- composeio/global/_bus/heartbeat                  |
|    +-- composeio/global/haven/governance/*              |
|    +-- ...                                              |
|                                                         |
|  Node-RED (:1880)                                       |
|    |                                                    |
|    +-- composeio-in nodes (MQTT subscribe)              |
|    +-- composeio-out nodes (MQTT publish)               |
|    +-- composeio-config node (shared MQTT connection    |
|    |   + namespace registry)                            |
|    +-- composeio-state-engine node (validation)         |
|    +-- composeio-log-query node (HTTP to bus storage)   |
|                                                         |
|  ComposeIO MCP Server (:8433)                           |
|  ComposeIO Bus Storage (HTTP API)                       |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
         |
    MQTT / HTTP
         |
+-----------------+       +------------------+
| David's Mac     |       | CAN Bus Devices  |
| ComposeUX       |       | (via CAN-MQTT    |
| Claude Code     |       |  bridge on RPi)  |
+-----------------+       +------------------+
```

Node-RED runs on `mqtt-broker.local` alongside the MQTT broker. This co-location
means MQTT connections are localhost — zero network latency, maximum throughput. The
Node-RED instance connects to `localhost:1883` just like the MCP server does
([MCP-ARCH-TOPOLOGY]).

---

## 2. Node-RED Configuration Node: `composeio-config` [NR-CONFIG]

### 2.1 Purpose [NR-CONFIG-PURPOSE]

A shared configuration node that manages the MQTT connection to the ComposeIO
broker and maintains a namespace registry. All ComposeIO nodes in a flow
reference a single `composeio-config` node instance.

This follows the Node-RED pattern where a config node (like `mqtt-broker`) is
shared across multiple working nodes.

### 2.2 Configuration Properties [NR-CONFIG-PROPS]

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `name` | string | `"ComposeIO Bus"` | Display name in the Node-RED editor |
| `broker` | mqtt-broker | (required) | Reference to a standard Node-RED `mqtt-broker` config node. The composeio-config node does NOT manage its own MQTT connection — it delegates transport to the built-in broker node |
| `clientId` | string | `"nodered-{random}"` | ComposeIO module identifier. Used as `src` in published messages. This is distinct from the mqtt-broker's clientId |
| `scope` | string | `"global"` | Default scope for published messages |
| `configPath` | string | `""` | Optional path to a `.compose.yaml` file. When set, the node loads namespace definitions and state engines from this file at startup |
| `busStorageUrl` | string | `""` | Optional URL of the ComposeIO bus storage HTTP API. When set, enables log queries and namespace registry sync |

### 2.3 Namespace Registry [NR-CONFIG-REGISTRY]

The config node maintains an in-memory namespace registry populated from two sources:

1. **Static config**: If `configPath` is set, parse the `.compose.yaml` and extract all namespace definitions (name, state engine type, values, label, mode). This gives the config node knowledge of the namespace contract before any messages arrive.

2. **Dynamic discovery**: The config node subscribes to `composeio/+/_bus/discovery` (MQTT wildcard). When discovery messages arrive ([BP-TYPE-DISCOVERY]), it extracts the `namespaces` field and merges into the registry. This captures namespaces from all bus participants — hardware modules, ComposeApps, the MCP server, other Node-RED instances.

The registry is available to all ComposeIO nodes via `node.composeioConfig.getNamespace(name)`. It returns:

```javascript
{
  name: "ftl_arm",
  label: "FTL Arm",
  stateEngine: {
    type: "binary",
    values: ["off", "on"],
    default: "off"
  },
  mode: "state",           // "state", "event_only", or "log"
  sources: ["panel-1"],    // known publishers from discovery
  lastValue: "on",         // last observed value (from retained MQTT)
  lastUpdated: 1742169600  // timestamp
}
```

### 2.4 MQTT Connection Management [NR-CONFIG-MQTT]

The config node references a standard Node-RED `mqtt-broker` config node for all
MQTT transport. It does NOT create its own MQTT connection. The built-in
mqtt-broker already handles connection lifecycle (reconnect, birth/will messages,
graceful shutdown with 2-second disconnect timeout), and the composeio-config
node benefits from this without reimplementing it.

The config node registers itself as a user of the mqtt-broker using the standard
`register()/deregister()` pattern ([NR-CONFIG-CONNMGR]). This ensures the broker
connection is active whenever ComposeIO nodes are deployed. Internal subscriptions
to `_bus/discovery` and `_bus/heartbeat` are added via the broker's subscription
management API — these are invisible to the flow developer and do not conflict
with other subscriptions on the same broker node.

Connection behavior (via the referenced mqtt-broker):
- Auto-reconnect is handled by the mqtt-broker config node
- On broker connect callback: subscribe to `composeio/+/_bus/discovery` for registry population
- On broker connect callback: subscribe to `composeio/+/_bus/heartbeat` for bus health monitoring
- Publish discovery announcement: `composeio/global/_bus/discovery` with Node-RED's capabilities

Flow developers who already have an `mqtt-broker` config node connected to the
same MQTT broker can reuse it — no duplicate TCP connection is created.

### 2.5 User Tracking: register/deregister Pattern [NR-CONFIG-USERS]

The composeio-config node implements the standard Node-RED config node user
tracking pattern ([NR-CONFIG-CONNMGR]):

- **`register(childNode)`**: Called by each `composeio-in`, `composeio-out`,
  `composeio-state-engine`, and `composeio-log-query` node during its
  initialization. When the first child node registers, the config node registers
  itself with the referenced `mqtt-broker` config node (triggering the MQTT
  connection if no other users exist) and subscribes to `_bus/discovery` and
  `_bus/heartbeat` in the broker's connect callback.

- **`deregister(childNode, done)`**: Called when a child node is removed or the
  flow is re-deployed. When the last child node deregisters, the config node
  deregisters from the mqtt-broker (allowing the broker to close the connection
  if no other users remain) and clears the namespace registry. The `done`
  callback is invoked after cleanup completes.

This pattern prevents resource leaks: no MQTT connection or bus subscriptions
exist when no ComposeIO nodes are deployed in the flow.

### 2.6 Discovery Announcement [NR-CONFIG-DISCOVERY]

On connection, the config node publishes a discovery message identifying Node-RED
as a bus participant:

```json
{
  "ns": "_bus/discovery",
  "type": "discovery",
  "val": {
    "module_id": "nodered-{clientId}",
    "role": "flow_engine",
    "hw": "node-red",
    "fw_ver": "1.0.0",
    "namespaces": {
      "publish": [],
      "subscribe": []
    },
    "capabilities": ["flow_engine", "http", "dashboard", "automation"]
  },
  "src": "nodered-{clientId}",
  "ts": 1742169600,
  "scope": "global",
  "seq": 1
}
```

The `publish` and `subscribe` arrays are populated dynamically as composeio-in and
composeio-out nodes are deployed. When a flow is deployed or updated, the config
node re-publishes discovery with the current namespace lists.

---

## 3. Input Node: `composeio-in` [NR-NODE-IN]

### 3.1 Purpose [NR-NODE-IN-PURPOSE]

Subscribes to a ComposeIO namespace and outputs messages to the flow when state
changes or events arrive. The node understands ComposeIO message semantics —
it parses the JSON payload, extracts typed fields, and presents them in
Node-RED's `msg` object model.

### 3.2 Configuration Properties [NR-NODE-IN-PROPS]

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `name` | string | `""` | Display name (auto-fills from namespace label if available) |
| `config` | composeio-config | (required) | Reference to shared config node |
| `namespace` | string | (required) | ComposeIO namespace to subscribe to. Supports MQTT-native wildcards: `+` matches any single topic level, `#` matches namespace subtrees. Examples: `composeio/+/+` subscribes to all namespaces on all scopes; `composeio/+/haven/#` subscribes to all `haven/` subtree namespaces. Glob-style partial wildcards (e.g., `engine_*`) are NOT supported — MQTT wildcards replace entire topic levels, not partial strings |
| `scope` | string | `"global"` | Scope filter: `"global"`, `"remote"`, or `"any"` (subscribes to all scopes) |
| `messageTypes` | string[] | `["state"]` | Which message types to pass through: `"state"`, `"event"`, `"log_entry"`, `"all"` |
| `outputFormat` | string | `"parsed"` | `"parsed"` (structured msg object) or `"raw"` (original JSON string as payload) |
| `onlyOnChange` | boolean | `true` | For state messages: only output when value differs from previous. Matches ComposeIO's on-change-only semantics [BP-TYPE-STATE] |

### 3.3 Output Message Format [NR-NODE-IN-OUTPUT]

When `outputFormat` is `"parsed"` (default), the node outputs:

```javascript
msg = {
  topic: "composeio/global/ftl_arm",     // original MQTT topic
  payload: "on",                          // the val field — the semantic value
  composeio: {
    namespace: "ftl_arm",
    type: "state",                        // message type
    value: "on",                          // same as payload, for explicit access
    source: "panel-1",
    timestamp: 1742169600,
    scope: "global",
    sequence: 42,
    label: "FTL Arm",                     // from namespace registry, if available
    stateEngine: {                        // from namespace registry, if available
      type: "binary",
      values: ["off", "on"]
    }
  }
}
```

**Design decision**: `msg.payload` is the ComposeIO value (`val` field), not the
full JSON message. This makes composeio-in nodes directly chainable with standard
Node-RED nodes (switch, change, template, dashboard) that operate on `msg.payload`.
The full ComposeIO metadata lives in `msg.composeio` for nodes that need it.

For event messages:
```javascript
msg = {
  topic: "composeio/global/ftl_arm",
  payload: "pressed",                     // event value
  composeio: {
    namespace: "ftl_arm",
    type: "event",
    value: "pressed",
    source: "panel-1",
    // ...
  }
}
```

For log_entry messages ([BP-EXT-LOG]):
```javascript
msg = {
  topic: "composeio/global/audit_trail",
  payload: { action: "widget_added", widget_id: "btn_001" },  // JSON object
  composeio: {
    namespace: "audit_trail",
    type: "log_entry",
    value: { action: "widget_added", widget_id: "btn_001" },
    source: "design_dispatcher",
    // ...
  }
}
```

### 3.4 Node Status Display [NR-NODE-IN-STATUS]

The node displays status in the Node-RED editor:

| State | Status Text | Color |
|-------|-------------|-------|
| Connected, no messages yet | `"connected"` | green ring |
| Connected, last value received | `"{value}"` (e.g., `"on"`, `"128"`) | green dot |
| Disconnected from broker | `"disconnected"` | red ring |
| Namespace not found in registry | `"unknown namespace"` | yellow ring |

### 3.5 MQTT Subscription [NR-NODE-IN-MQTT]

The node subscribes to MQTT topic:

```
composeio/{scope}/{namespace}
```

Where `{scope}` is the configured scope or `+` (MQTT single-level wildcard) when
scope is `"any"`. The namespace field supports MQTT-native wildcards directly:
`+` matches any single namespace level and `#` matches namespace subtrees. No
glob-to-MQTT translation is performed — the namespace value is used as-is in the
MQTT topic subscription.

QoS follows the ComposeIO spec ([BP-MQTT-QOS]):
- State subscriptions: QoS 1 (to receive retained messages)
- Event subscriptions: QoS 0

---

## 4. Output Node: `composeio-out` [NR-NODE-OUT]

### 4.1 Purpose [NR-NODE-OUT-PURPOSE]

Publishes messages to a ComposeIO namespace with proper message formatting.
The node constructs a valid ComposeIO bus message ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]) from the
incoming `msg` object, validating against the state engine if known.

### 4.2 Configuration Properties [NR-NODE-OUT-PROPS]

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `name` | string | `""` | Display name |
| `config` | composeio-config | (required) | Reference to shared config node |
| `namespace` | string | `""` | Target namespace. If empty, uses `msg.composeio.namespace` or `msg.topic` (parsed) |
| `messageType` | string | `"state"` | `"state"`, `"event"`, or `"log_entry"` |
| `scope` | string | `"global"` | Scope for published messages |
| `source` | string | `""` | Source module ID. Default: config node's clientId |
| `validate` | boolean | `true` | Validate value against state engine before publishing. If validation fails, the node outputs on its second output (error port) instead of publishing |

### 4.3 Input Message Handling [NR-NODE-OUT-INPUT]

The node accepts `msg.payload` as the value to publish. It constructs the
full ComposeIO message:

```javascript
// Incoming msg:
msg = { payload: "on" }

// Published MQTT message on composeio/global/ftl_arm:
{
  "ns": "ftl_arm",
  "type": "state",
  "val": "on",
  "src": "nodered-main",
  "ts": 1742169600,
  "scope": "global",
  "seq": 43
}
```

If the incoming `msg` has a `msg.composeio` object, the node uses those fields
as overrides (namespace, type, scope, source). This enables chaining
composeio-in -> processing -> composeio-out where the metadata flows through.

### 4.4 State Engine Validation [NR-NODE-OUT-VALIDATE]

When `validate` is true and the namespace's state engine is known (from the
config node's registry):

1. Look up the state engine for the target namespace
2. Check if `msg.payload` is a valid value for that engine:
   - `binary`: must be `"off"` or `"on"`
   - `tri_level`: must be `"off"`, `"half"`, or `"full"`
   - `stop_light`: must be `"green"`, `"yellow"`, or `"red"`
   - `continuous_0_255`: must be an integer 0-255
   - `enum`: must be a member of the defined values list
   - `json`: must be valid JSON ([BP-EXT-JSON])
3. If valid: publish to MQTT
4. If invalid: do NOT publish. Send the message to the node's error output
   (second output port) with `msg.error = { message: "Invalid value 'xyz' for state engine binary on namespace ftl_arm", validValues: ["off", "on"] }`

This prevents malformed values from reaching the bus — a guardrail that raw
MQTT nodes lack.

### 4.5 Sequence Number Management [NR-NODE-OUT-SEQ]

The config node maintains a per-source rolling sequence counter. Each
composeio-out node increments the sequence on each publish. The counter wraps
at 65535 per [BP-MSG-FIELDS].

### 4.6 QoS and Retain [NR-NODE-OUT-QOS]

QoS and retain flags follow [BP-MQTT-QOS]:

| Message Type | QoS | Retain |
|-------------|-----|--------|
| `state` | 1 | Yes |
| `event` | 0 | No |
| `log_entry` | 1 | No |

### 4.7 Node Status Display [NR-NODE-OUT-STATUS]

| State | Status Text | Color |
|-------|-------------|-------|
| Connected, idle | `"connected"` | green ring |
| Last published value | `"-> {value}"` (e.g., `"-> on"`) | green dot |
| Validation error | `"invalid: {value}"` | red dot |
| Disconnected | `"disconnected"` | red ring |

---

## 5. Validation Node: `composeio-state-engine` [NR-NODE-ENGINE]

### 5.1 Purpose [NR-NODE-ENGINE-PURPOSE]

A standalone validation node that checks whether `msg.payload` is valid for a
given state engine. Useful in flows where processing generates values that
need validation before reaching a composeio-out node, or where validation
results drive branching logic.

### 5.2 Configuration Properties [NR-NODE-ENGINE-PROPS]

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `name` | string | `""` | Display name |
| `config` | composeio-config | (required) | Reference to shared config node |
| `namespace` | string | `""` | Namespace to validate against. If empty, uses `msg.composeio.namespace` |
| `engineOverride` | object | `null` | Explicit state engine definition (overrides registry lookup). Useful when the namespace isn't in the registry yet |

### 5.3 Behavior [NR-NODE-ENGINE-BEHAVIOR]

Two outputs:
- **Output 1 (valid)**: Message passes through unchanged if `msg.payload` is valid for the state engine
- **Output 2 (invalid)**: Message routed here if validation fails. `msg.error` is added with details

Additionally, the node enriches `msg.composeio.stateEngine` with the engine
definition if not already present. This enables downstream nodes to make
state-aware decisions.

### 5.4 State Advance Function [NR-NODE-ENGINE-ADVANCE]

When `msg.payload` is the special string `"__advance"`, the node computes the
next value in the state engine's value cycle and sets `msg.payload` to that value.
This enables flows that implement toggle behavior without hardcoding state values:

```
[inject: "__advance"] -> [composeio-state-engine: ftl_arm] -> [composeio-out: ftl_arm]
```

The node reads the current value from the config node's namespace registry
(`lastValue`), advances to the next value in the engine's value list (wrapping),
and outputs the new value. This mirrors the firmware's toggle behavior in a
flow-native way.

---

## 6. Log Query Node: `composeio-log-query` [NR-NODE-LOG]

### 6.1 Purpose [NR-NODE-LOG-PURPOSE]

Queries the ComposeIO bus storage HTTP API for log namespace history
([BP-EXT-LOG]). Returns past entries from `log` mode namespaces. This node
is not MQTT-based — it makes HTTP requests to the bus storage REST API.

### 6.2 Configuration Properties [NR-NODE-LOG-PROPS]

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `name` | string | `""` | Display name |
| `config` | composeio-config | (required) | Reference to shared config node (uses `busStorageUrl`) |
| `namespace` | string | (required) | Log namespace to query |
| `since` | string | `""` | Timestamp filter. Supports `msg.since` override. Empty = all entries |
| `limit` | integer | `100` | Maximum entries to return |

### 6.3 Output [NR-NODE-LOG-OUTPUT]

```javascript
msg = {
  payload: [
    { val: { action: "widget_added", widget_id: "btn_001" }, src: "designer", ts: 12345, seq: 50 },
    { val: { action: "widget_moved", widget_id: "btn_001" }, src: "designer", ts: 12346, seq: 51 }
  ],
  composeio: {
    namespace: "audit_trail",
    total: 142,
    hasMore: true
  }
}
```

The API endpoint is `GET {busStorageUrl}/api/v1/log/{namespace}?since={since}&limit={limit}`
per [BP-EXT-LOG].

---

## 7. Namespace Browser Node: `composeio-browse` [NR-NODE-BROWSE]

### 7.1 Purpose [NR-NODE-BROWSE-PURPOSE]

An editor-only sidebar panel (Node-RED sidebar plugin) that displays the live
namespace registry from the config node. Flow developers use this to discover
available namespaces, their state engines, current values, and labels without
leaving the Node-RED editor.

### 7.2 Features [NR-NODE-BROWSE-FEATURES]

- **Live namespace list**: Shows all namespaces from the registry, grouped by source module
- **State engine display**: Shows the type and valid values for each namespace
- **Current value**: Shows the last observed value (from MQTT retained messages)
- **Click-to-copy**: Clicking a namespace copies its name to the clipboard for pasting into node configuration fields
- **Create Node button**: A "Create Node" button on each namespace entry adds a pre-configured `composeio-in` node to the current flow via the Admin API
- **Search/filter**: Filter namespaces by name, label, state engine type, or source
- **Bus health**: Shows MQTT connection status, bus master heartbeat, and connected module count
- **Stretch goal — Drag-to-flow**: Dragging a namespace from the browser onto the flow canvas creates a pre-configured `composeio-in` node. This requires undocumented editor internals and may break across Node-RED versions; implement only if the documented Admin API approach proves insufficient

---

## 8. Message Object Model Mapping [NR-MSG-MODEL]

### 8.1 ComposeIO to Node-RED Mapping [NR-MSG-CIO-TO-NR]

The mapping between ComposeIO bus messages and Node-RED's `msg` object:

| ComposeIO Field | `msg` Property | Notes |
|-----------------|----------------|-------|
| `ns` | `msg.composeio.namespace` | Also used to construct `msg.topic` |
| `type` | `msg.composeio.type` | |
| `val` | `msg.payload` | The primary value — directly usable by standard nodes |
| `src` | `msg.composeio.source` | |
| `ts` | `msg.composeio.timestamp` | |
| `scope` | `msg.composeio.scope` | |
| `seq` | `msg.composeio.sequence` | |
| `ttl` | `msg.composeio.ttl` | Only if present |
| `pri` | `msg.composeio.priority` | Only if present |
| `cid` | `msg.composeio.correlationId` | Only if present |
| (MQTT topic) | `msg.topic` | Full MQTT topic: `composeio/{scope}/{ns}` |

### 8.2 Node-RED to ComposeIO Mapping [NR-MSG-NR-TO-CIO]

When composeio-out publishes, it constructs the bus message from:

| `msg` Property | ComposeIO Field | Fallback |
|----------------|-----------------|----------|
| `msg.payload` | `val` | (required) |
| `msg.composeio.namespace` | `ns` | Node's configured namespace |
| `msg.composeio.type` | `type` | Node's configured messageType |
| `msg.composeio.scope` | `scope` | Node's configured scope |
| `msg.composeio.source` | `src` | Config node's clientId |
| (auto-generated) | `ts` | Current unix timestamp |
| (auto-incremented) | `seq` | Config node's sequence counter |

---

## 9. Common Flow Patterns [NR-PATTERNS]

### 9.1 Namespace-to-Namespace Binding [NR-PATTERN-BINDING]

The simplest ComposeIO pattern in Node-RED: wire one namespace to another with
a transform in between.

```
[composeio-in: laser_power] -> [function: threshold map] -> [composeio-out: vent_fan_speed]
```

This replicates a ComposeIO binding ([CS-BINDINGS]) but with the full power of
Node-RED's function nodes for the transform. Use this when the transform is too
complex for ComposeIO's declarative binding syntax ([DP-CIO-10]), or when you
need side effects (logging, notifications, HTTP calls) during the transform.

**When to use Node-RED bindings vs ComposeIO config bindings**: If the binding
is a simple map/threshold/scale that needs to survive host failure, declare it
in `.compose.yaml` and let the bus handle it ([DP-CIO-11]). If the binding needs
external API calls, conditional logic based on time/history, or integration with
non-bus systems, use a Node-RED flow.

### 9.2 HA Entity Bridge [NR-PATTERN-HA-BRIDGE]

Bridge a Home Assistant entity to a ComposeIO namespace using Node-RED's
`node-red-contrib-home-assistant-websocket` nodes:

```
[ha-entity: sensor.office_temperature] -> [change: scale] -> [composeio-out: office_temp]
[composeio-in: office_heater] -> [ha-service: climate.set_temperature]
```

This is the lightweight alternative to a full ComposeIO HA adapter ([DP-CIO-20])
for one-off integrations that don't justify a dedicated adapter.

### 9.3 Dashboard with ComposeIO State [NR-PATTERN-DASHBOARD]

Use Node-RED Dashboard 2.0 nodes fed by composeio-in nodes:

```
[composeio-in: engine_rpm] -> [ui-gauge: RPM]
[composeio-in: test_progress] -> [ui-chart: Progress Over Time]
[ui-button: Start] -> [composeio-out: dashboard_cmd (val: "start")]
```

Because `msg.payload` carries the ComposeIO value directly, dashboard nodes
work without any adapter functions.

### 9.4 Notification on State Change [NR-PATTERN-NOTIFY]

```
[composeio-in: engine_rpm] -> [switch: val > 5000]
    -> [email/pushover/telegram node]
```

Node-RED's notification ecosystem (email, Pushover, Telegram, Slack, Discord)
becomes available for any ComposeIO bus event. Subscribe to any namespace and
use Node-RED's switch/function nodes to filter for conditions worth alerting on.

### 9.5 Capture-to-Database [NR-PATTERN-DB]

```
[composeio-in: engine_rpm (messageTypes: all)] -> [function: format]
    -> [influxdb-out: composeio_metrics]
```

Feed ComposeIO namespace traffic into InfluxDB, PostgreSQL, or any database
Node-RED supports. The `composeio` metadata object provides namespace, source,
and timestamp for structured storage.

### 9.6 HTTP API for ComposeIO State [NR-PATTERN-HTTP]

```
[http-in: GET /api/composeio/:namespace] -> [function: lookup registry]
    -> [http-response: 200 JSON]

[http-in: POST /api/composeio/:namespace] -> [composeio-state-engine]
    -> [composeio-out] -> [http-response: 200]
    |-> (invalid) -> [http-response: 400 error]
```

Expose ComposeIO namespaces as a REST API. The state engine validation node
gates incoming values before they reach the bus — invalid requests get a 400
response without polluting the bus.

---

## 10. Deployment [NR-DEPLOY]

### 10.1 Target Environment [NR-DEPLOY-TARGET]

Node-RED runs on `mqtt-broker.local` (Ubuntu server with Docker and K3s).
The MQTT broker is already on the same host at port 1883.

### 10.2 Docker Deployment [NR-DEPLOY-DOCKER]

```yaml
# docker-compose.yml
services:
  node-red:
    image: nodered/node-red:latest
    ports:
      - "1880:1880"
    volumes:
      - nodered-data:/data
      - /opt/composeio/configs:/composeio/configs:ro  # .compose.yaml files
    environment:
      - TZ=America/Los_Angeles
    restart: always
    network_mode: host    # localhost MQTT access (same pattern as MCP server)

volumes:
  nodered-data:
```

Using `network_mode: host` provides localhost access to the MQTT broker at
port 1883 without Docker networking overhead. This matches the MCP server's
deployment pattern ([MCP-DEPLOY-DOCKER]).

### 10.3 K8s Deployment (Alternative) [NR-DEPLOY-K8S]

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: node-red
  namespace: composeio
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-red
  template:
    spec:
      hostNetwork: true
      containers:
        - name: node-red
          image: nodered/node-red:latest
          ports:
            - containerPort: 1880
          volumeMounts:
            - name: data
              mountPath: /data
            - name: configs
              mountPath: /composeio/configs
              readOnly: true
      volumes:
        - name: data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: nodered-pvc
        - name: configs
          hostPath:
            path: /opt/composeio/configs
```

### 10.4 Custom Node Installation [NR-DEPLOY-NODES]

The ComposeIO custom nodes are packaged as a single npm module:
`@composeio/node-red-contrib-composeio`.

```bash
cd /data  # Node-RED user directory
npm install @composeio/node-red-contrib-composeio
```

Or via the Node-RED Palette Manager UI.

The package includes:
- `composeio-config` (configuration node)
- `composeio-in` (input node)
- `composeio-out` (output node)
- `composeio-state-engine` (validation node)
- `composeio-log-query` (log query node)
- `composeio-browse` (sidebar plugin)

### 10.5 Migration from David's Mac [NR-DEPLOY-MIGRATION]

Node-RED is currently running on David's Mac. Migration to mqtt-broker.local:

1. Export all flows from David's Mac: `GET http://localhost:1880/flows` (or use the Node-RED export UI)
2. Deploy Node-RED container on mqtt-broker.local per section 10.2
3. Import flows: `POST http://mqtt-broker.local:1880/flows`
4. Update any `mqtt-broker` config nodes in existing flows to point to `localhost:1883` (since Node-RED will now be co-located with the broker)
5. Install `@composeio/node-red-contrib-composeio` on the new instance
6. Gradually migrate existing MQTT-based flows to use composeio-in/out nodes for state engine awareness and validation
7. Verify all flows are operational, then decommission Node-RED on the Mac

Existing flows that use raw MQTT nodes with `composeio/` topics will continue
to work without modification — the custom nodes are additive, not a replacement
for raw MQTT access.

---

## 11. Bus Participation Contract [NR-BUS-CONTRACT]

### 11.1 Node-RED as a Bus Citizen [NR-BUS-CITIZEN]

When the `composeio-config` node is connected, Node-RED is a full ComposeIO bus
participant with the same obligations as any other participant:

| Obligation | Implementation |
|------------|----------------|
| Discovery announcement | Config node publishes to `_bus/discovery` on connect and every 60s |
| Heartbeat | Config node publishes to `_bus/heartbeat/nodered-{id}` every 30s |
| Source identification | All published messages carry `src: "nodered-{clientId}"` |
| Scope compliance | Messages respect configured scope; `auto` is resolved per [BP-NS-AUTO-RESOLVE] |
| Message format | All published messages conform to [BP-MSG-FIELDS] |
| Size constraint | All messages under 256 bytes ([BP-MSG-SIZE]) |
| Deduplication | Incoming messages deduplicated by `(src, seq)` per [BP-SCOPE-DEDUP] |

### 11.2 What Node-RED Does NOT Do [NR-BUS-LIMITATIONS]

Node-RED's integration is MQTT-only. It does not:

- Act as a bus master (no time_sync publishing, no config_push authority)
- Participate in CAN bus directly (CAN traffic reaches Node-RED via the CAN-MQTT bridge)
- Implement a critical ack protocol as an acker (it can observe ack messages but does not respond to `pri: critical` messages with acks)
- Enforce namespace access policies ([BP-EXT-ACCESS]) at the Node-RED level (access control is enforced by the bus runtime, not by individual participants)
- Replace the ComposeIO Python bus library for Python applications (use the library directly for ComposeApps; use Node-RED for flow-based integration and glue)

---

## 12. npm Package Structure [NR-PACKAGE]

### 12.1 Package Layout [NR-PACKAGE-LAYOUT]

```
@composeio/node-red-contrib-composeio/
  package.json
  nodes/
    composeio-config.js        # Configuration node
    composeio-config.html      # Editor UI for config node
    composeio-in.js            # Input node
    composeio-in.html          # Editor UI with namespace autocomplete
    composeio-out.js           # Output node
    composeio-out.html         # Editor UI with state engine display
    composeio-state-engine.js  # Validation node
    composeio-state-engine.html
    composeio-log-query.js     # Log query node
    composeio-log-query.html
    composeio-browse.js        # Sidebar plugin
    composeio-browse.html      # Sidebar UI
  lib/
    state-engines.js           # State engine validation logic (JS port of engine types)
    message-builder.js         # ComposeIO message construction utility
    yaml-parser.js             # .compose.yaml parser for namespace extraction
  test/
    composeio-in_spec.js
    composeio-out_spec.js
    state-engines_spec.js
    message-builder_spec.js
```

### 12.2 Dependencies [NR-PACKAGE-DEPS]

```json
{
  "name": "@composeio/node-red-contrib-composeio",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "description": "ComposeIO bus integration nodes for Node-RED",
  "node-red": {
    "version": ">=2.0.0",
    "nodes": {
      "composeio-config": "nodes/composeio-config.js",
      "composeio-in": "nodes/composeio-in.js",
      "composeio-out": "nodes/composeio-out.js",
      "composeio-state-engine": "nodes/composeio-state-engine.js",
      "composeio-log-query": "nodes/composeio-log-query.js",
      "composeio-browse": "nodes/composeio-browse.js"
    }
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "js-yaml": "^4.1.0"
  }
}
```

No `mqtt` npm dependency is needed — the composeio-config node delegates all MQTT
transport to Node-RED's built-in `mqtt-broker` config node (see [NR-CONFIG-MQTT]).
`js-yaml` parses `.compose.yaml` files for namespace extraction.

**Version constraint note**: Requires Node-RED 2.0+ for env var access
(`NR_*` environment variables added in 2.2) and modern editor APIs.

---

## 13. Compliance Requirements [NR-COMPLIANCE]

A compliant ComposeIO Node-RED integration MUST:

1. **[NR-C1]** Publish all messages in valid ComposeIO JSON format with all seven mandatory fields ([BP-MSG-FIELDS])
2. **[NR-C2]** Use the MQTT topic format `composeio/{scope}/{namespace}` per [BP-MQTT-TOPICS]
3. **[NR-C3]** Follow QoS and retain mapping per [BP-MQTT-QOS] for all published message types
4. **[NR-C4]** Publish discovery announcements to `_bus/discovery` on connection and every 60 seconds
5. **[NR-C5]** Publish heartbeat to `_bus/heartbeat/{module_id}` every 30 seconds
6. **[NR-C6]** Include a valid `src` field on all published messages identifying the Node-RED instance
7. **[NR-C7]** Maintain a rolling 16-bit sequence counter per source, wrapping at 65535
8. **[NR-C8]** The `composeio-in` node MUST present `msg.payload` as the ComposeIO `val` field, with full metadata in `msg.composeio`
9. **[NR-C9]** The `composeio-out` node MUST validate values against the state engine when validation is enabled and the engine is known
10. **[NR-C10]** All published messages MUST be under 256 bytes ([BP-MSG-SIZE])
11. **[NR-C11]** The `composeio-config` node MUST subscribe to `_bus/discovery` and maintain a namespace registry
12. **[NR-C12]** The `composeio-out` node MUST NOT publish to `_bus/` infrastructure namespaces via flow wiring. Infrastructure namespace publishing (`_bus/discovery`, `_bus/heartbeat`) is handled internally by the `composeio-config` node as part of bus participation and is not accessible to flow developers

A compliant implementation SHOULD:

13. **[NR-S1]** Support namespace wildcard subscriptions in `composeio-in` via MQTT wildcards
14. **[NR-S2]** Support loading namespace definitions from `.compose.yaml` files
15. **[NR-S3]** Provide the namespace browser sidebar panel for live discovery
16. **[NR-S4]** Support the `__advance` state engine function in `composeio-state-engine`
17. **[NR-S5]** Support `log_entry` message type for `log` mode namespaces ([BP-EXT-LOG])
18. **[NR-S6]** Deduplicate incoming messages by `(src, seq)` per [BP-SCOPE-DEDUP]

---

## 14. Implementation Priority [NR-IMPL-PRIORITY]

### Build Together (single implementation pass)

| Node | Rationale |
|------|-----------|
| `composeio-config` | Foundation — all other nodes depend on it |
| `composeio-in` | Read from bus |
| `composeio-out` | Write to bus with message formatting |
| `composeio-state-engine` | Pure validation logic — no dependency on seeing core nodes "proven" first |

These four nodes are independent enough to build in parallel and have no evaluation gate between them. State engine validation is not a "phase 2" add-on — it is core to the value proposition (preventing malformed values from reaching the bus). Building it separately creates a window where flows run without validation, which is strictly worse.

### Deferred: Evaluation Gate

| Node | Gate | Rationale |
|------|------|-----------|
| `composeio-browse` | Real flow usage feedback | Editor sidebar UX benefits from seeing how flows feel in practice before investing in drag-to-flow and live registry display. Build after the core nodes are used in at least one real flow. |

### Deferred: External Dependency

| Node | Dependency | Rationale |
|------|------------|-----------|
| `composeio-log-query` | `bus_storage` HTTP API deployed and validated | Cannot test without the API endpoint. Build when bus_storage is running on mqtt-broker.local. |

